1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,760
This is ECE 358, video 16 a. The topic is packet forwarding and longest prefix matching. It covers slides 49 to 58 of chapters 4 and 5.

2
00:00:14,210 --> 00:00:29,229
After this video, you can read a routing table. You can test if a destination address matches a table entry. And you can choose the output interface with longest prefix matching. The question bank asks all of these.

3
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:44,825
Packet forwarding is the relaying of packets from one physical interface to another, by routers on the Internet. Here, packet P arrives at router B. Router B has three interfaces, numbered 1, 2 and 3.

4
00:00:45,275 --> 00:00:58,011
How does router B choose the next hop for P? The answer is a function of two inputs. The first input is the destination address in P. The second input is the routing table in B.

5
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:08,488
The router reads the destination IP address from the header of P. Here, the destination is 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9.

6
00:01:08,938 --> 00:01:20,287
The router looks up this address in its routing table. The table says: send it on interface 1, to the next router, I P 1. So P leaves on interface 1.

7
00:01:20,737 --> 00:01:29,996
This method is called table-driven routing. The routing table decides the output interface. The next slides explain the function f.

8
00:01:30,446 --> 00:01:42,073
Slide 50 also names another forwarding technique: source routing. Here, the source host finds the complete path. It puts the path in the header, in the option field.

9
00:01:42,523 --> 00:01:49,776
The source sends the packet to the first router of the path, R 1. R 1 reads the path in the packet.

10
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:57,565
R 1 sends the packet to the next router of the path. Each router does the same, until the packet arrives.

11
00:01:58,015 --> 00:02:05,161
In this course, routers use table-driven routing. Source routing is only an example of another technique.

12
00:02:05,611 --> 00:02:25,323
Slide 51 shows the simple structure of a routing table, for router B. Each row has five columns. The destination address and the mask give a destination network. The next hop is the next router. The interface is the output port of B. The metric is the cost of the route.

13
00:02:25,773 --> 00:02:51,949
Row 1: destination 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, mask 255 dot 255 dot 0 dot 0. That is the network 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, slash 16. The next hop is router I P 1, on interface 1. B learns this entry by running routing protocols. The star marks it as learned.

14
00:02:52,399 --> 00:03:12,623
Row 2: the network 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 0, slash 24, on interface 3. The next hop is connected. This means that the network is directly attached to B. B delivers the packet to the host directly. The administrator configures this entry.

15
00:03:13,073 --> 00:03:33,553
Row 3 is the default entry: address 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0 with mask 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0. The next hop is I P 2, on interface 2. This mask has no 1 bits, so this entry matches every address. The administrator configures it.

16
00:03:34,003 --> 00:03:54,889
Here are three example packets at router B. These addresses are my examples. They are not on the slide. First, a packet to 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9. Its first 16 bits are 73 dot 2. So it matches row 1, and it leaves on interface 1, to I P 1.

17
00:03:55,339 --> 00:04:12,619
Second, a packet to 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 20. Its first 24 bits are 129 dot 97 dot 8. So it matches the connected row. B sends it on interface 3, directly to the host.

18
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:24,631
Third, a packet to 8 dot 8 dot 8 dot 8. It matches neither network. It matches only the default entry. So it leaves on interface 2, to I P 2.

19
00:04:25,081 --> 00:04:35,599
So a packet to a connected network goes directly to the host. Every other packet goes to a next-hop router. Next, we look at the exact matching test.

20
00:04:36,049 --> 00:05:10,965
How does the router test if a destination address matches one entry? Slide 53 gives the condition. Take the entry address x dot y dot z dot w and its mask a dot b dot c dot d. The mask has n one bits, so the entry is x dot y dot z dot w slash n. Here, the entry is 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0, with mask 255 dot 255 dot 0 dot 0. So n is 16.

21
00:05:11,415 --> 00:05:22,423
Take the destination address from the packet. Call it I P 1. Here, I P 1 is 73 dot 2 dot 5 dot 9. Use the same mask.

22
00:05:22,873 --> 00:05:42,159
Do a bitwise AND with the mask on both sides. A mask byte of 255 keeps the byte. A mask byte of 0 makes it 0. The entry gives 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0. I P 1 also gives 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0.

23
00:05:42,609 --> 00:05:48,518
The two results are equal. So the matching is successful. The packet can use this entry.

24
00:05:48,968 --> 00:06:05,075
Now try I P 1 equal to 73 dot 3 dot 5 dot 9. The AND gives 73 dot 3 dot 0 dot 0. This is not equal to 73 dot 2 dot 0 dot 0. So there is no match.

25
00:06:05,525 --> 00:06:18,794
In words: the test asks if I P 1 is in the pool of addresses that x dot y dot z dot w slash n denotes. It compares the n most significant bits of both addresses.

26
00:06:19,244 --> 00:06:39,745
Why does the routing table store the prefix length as a mask, in the format of an IP address? A slash 24 prefix becomes 24 one bits, then 8 zero bits. In dotted form, that is 255 dot 255 dot 255 dot 0.

27
00:06:40,195 --> 00:07:00,163
To test an address, the router does one AND with the mask. Here, I P 1 is 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 20. The AND keeps the first 24 bits and clears the last 8 bits. The result is 129 dot 97 dot 8 dot 0.

28
00:07:00,613 --> 00:07:19,067
The mask is two consecutive blocks of ones and zeros. So one AND operation gives the network part of any address. The slide says that this helps an efficient implementation of the matching condition. This is the answer to question bank question 65.

29
00:07:19,517 --> 00:07:36,669
Slide 54 gives the full matching and forwarding algorithm. The inputs are I P 1, the destination address from the packet header, and R T, the routing table. First, the router tests I P 1 against every entry of the table.

30
00:07:37,119 --> 00:07:51,263
If one or more entries match, the router finds the matching entry with the longest prefix. It forwards the packet on the interface of that entry. The longest prefix takes the packet closer to the destination network.

31
00:07:51,713 --> 00:08:05,174
If no entry matches, the router checks for a default entry, 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0 slash 0. If it exists, the router forwards the packet on the default interface.

32
00:08:05,624 --> 00:08:21,773
If there is no match and no default entry, the router cannot forward the packet. It sends an error message to the source of the packet. This is an I C M P message. I C M P is the Internet Control Message Protocol.

33
00:08:22,223 --> 00:08:59,444
Now the main rule. Slide 55 gives a table in binary. A star is a bit that can be anything. Entry 0 fixes the first 21 bits, so it is slash 21. Entry 1 fixes 24 bits, slash 24. Entry 2 fixes 21 bits, slash 21. Entry 3 is the default. The rule: use the longest prefix that matches the destination. The first destination is 200 dot 23 dot 22 dot 161. Pause the video and try it.

34
00:08:59,894 --> 00:09:17,046
Entry 0: compare the first 21 bits. The third byte of the destination is 0 0 0 1 0 1 1 0. Its first 5 bits are 0 0 0 1 0, the same as the entry. So entry 0 matches.

35
00:09:17,496 --> 00:09:32,195
Entry 1: compare the first 24 bits. The third byte must be 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 0, which is 24. The destination has 22. So entry 1 does not match.

36
00:09:32,645 --> 00:09:44,698
Entry 2: the third byte must start with 0 0 0 1 1. The destination starts with 0 0 0 1 0. The fifth bit differs. No match.

37
00:09:45,148 --> 00:09:51,633
The default entry matches every address. But it has prefix length 0, the shortest possible.

38
00:09:52,083 --> 00:10:02,067
Only entry 0 and the default match. Entry 0 has the longer prefix, 21 bits. So the packet goes out on interface 0.

39
00:10:02,517 --> 00:10:10,987
The second destination is 200 dot 23 dot 24 dot 170. Pause the video and try it.

40
00:10:11,437 --> 00:10:25,495
Entry 0: the third byte must start with 0 0 0 1 0. The destination third byte is 0 0 0 1 1 0 0 0. The fifth bit differs. No match.

41
00:10:25,945 --> 00:10:36,953
Entry 1: the first 24 bits must be 200 dot 23 dot 24. The destination has exactly these bits. Entry 1 matches.

42
00:10:37,403 --> 00:10:48,134
Entry 2: the third byte must start with 0 0 0 1 1. It does. So entry 2 also matches. Slide 57 shows this match.

43
00:10:48,584 --> 00:10:51,571
The default also matches, with length 0.

44
00:10:52,021 --> 00:11:16,597
Three entries match. Entry 1 has 24 bits, entry 2 has 21 bits, the default has 0. The longest is entry 1, as slide 58 shows. So the packet goes out on interface 1. Entry 1 is a smaller, more specific network inside the range of entry 2. The longer prefix takes the packet closer to the destination network.

45
00:11:17,047 --> 00:11:35,777
Practice with the same table. These two addresses are my examples, not from the slide. The first address is 200 dot 23 dot 31 dot 5. The third byte, 31, is 0 0 0 1 1 1 1 1. Pause the video and try it.

46
00:11:36,227 --> 00:11:42,777
Entry 0 needs the third byte to start with 0 0 0 1 0. No match.

47
00:11:43,227 --> 00:11:49,840
Entry 1 needs the third byte equal to 24. The address has 31. No match.

48
00:11:50,290 --> 00:12:00,466
Entry 2 needs the third byte to start with 0 0 0 1 1. 31 starts with 0 0 0 1 1. Match.

49
00:12:00,916 --> 00:12:02,473
The default matches too.

50
00:12:02,923 --> 00:12:12,950
Entry 2, slash 21, is the longest match. So the answer is interface 2. Entry 2 covers third bytes 24 to 31.

51
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:25,325
The second address is 200 dot 23 dot 40 dot 1. The third byte, 40, is 0 0 1 0 1 0 0 0. Pause and try it.

52
00:12:25,775 --> 00:12:32,197
Entry 0: the third bit of the third byte is 1, but the entry needs 0. No match.

53
00:12:32,647 --> 00:12:35,996
Entry 1: 40 is not 24. No match.

54
00:12:36,446 --> 00:12:39,945
Entry 2: the same third bit differs. No match.

55
00:12:40,395 --> 00:12:42,315
Only the default matches.

56
00:12:42,765 --> 00:12:53,559
So the packet goes out on interface 3, the default interface. Without the default entry, the router would drop it and send an I C M P error message.

57
00:12:54,009 --> 00:13:07,471
Now the question bank questions on forwarding. Questions 57 and 58: what is table-driven routing, and what two pieces of information does a router use? Pause the video and try it.

58
00:13:07,921 --> 00:13:21,617
Table-driven routing means that the router finds the next hop by a lookup in its routing table. The two pieces of information are the destination IP address in the packet, and the routing table in the router.

59
00:13:22,067 --> 00:13:29,896
Questions 59 and 61: show the template of a routing table, and explain its structure. Pause and try it.

60
00:13:30,346 --> 00:13:50,677
Each row has five fields. The destination address and the mask together give a destination network, x dot y dot z dot w slash n. The next hop is the next router, or connected for a network attached to the router. The interface is the output port. The metric is the cost.

61
00:13:51,127 --> 00:13:56,951
Question 60: explain configured, learned and default entries. Pause and try it.

62
00:13:57,401 --> 00:14:16,601
A configured entry is typed in by the administrator, for example a network connected to the router. A learned entry comes from routing protocols. A default entry is 0 dot 0 dot 0 dot 0 slash 0. It is configured, and it matches every address.

63
00:14:17,051 --> 00:14:22,512
Question 62 has three parts on prefix matching. Pause the video and try it.

64
00:14:22,962 --> 00:14:40,711
Part a. An entry x dot y dot z dot w slash n matches I P 1 if the AND with the mask gives the same result for both. That is, the n most significant bits are equal. Then I P 1 is in the pool of addresses of that entry.

65
00:14:41,161 --> 00:14:48,052
Part b. Longest prefix matching: among all entries that match, take the one with the largest n.

66
00:14:48,502 --> 00:15:01,643
Part c. The largest n is the most specific network. It takes the packet closest to the destination network. The router forwards the packet on the interface of that entry, to its next hop.

67
00:15:02,093 --> 00:15:11,117
Questions 63 and 64: what happens if no entry matches, and why is a default entry useful? Pause and try it.

68
00:15:11,567 --> 00:15:37,893
If no entry matches and there is no default entry, the router drops the packet and sends an I C M P error message to the source. A default entry sends every unknown destination to one next hop, for example the router of the I S P. So the table stays small, and packets to unknown networks are not dropped. The last two reasons are my reasoning from slide 54.

69
00:15:38,343 --> 00:15:56,305
To recap. The next hop is a function of the destination address and the routing table. Each row has a destination, a mask, a next hop, an interface and a metric. Entries are learned from routing protocols, or configured: connected networks and the default.

70
00:15:56,755 --> 00:16:11,987
An entry matches if the AND with its mask gives the same result for the entry and the destination. The longest matching prefix wins. With no match and no default, the router drops the packet and sends an I C M P error.

71
00:16:12,437 --> 00:16:16,427
The next video, 16 b, covers address aggregation.
